    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d818">

                <pb facs="1040" n="926" sameAs="frus:frus1922v02/pg926"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867.4016/534b</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        President Harding</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-05-27"><hi rendition="#italic">May 27,
                        1922</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. President</hi>: Referring to our
                    conversation today with respect to the proposed inquiry into the atrocities in
                    Anatolia, I take the liberty of enclosing a copy of a letter addressed by Dr.
                    Ward and Mr. Yowell to the American Consul at Aleppo, under date of April 5,
                        1922,<anchor n="10" xml:id="d818-f1"/> and also a copy of Dr. Barton’s
                    letter addressed to me under date of May 19, 1922.<anchor n="11"
                        xml:id="d818-f2"/> I also enclose the report of the statement of Mr. Austen
                    Chamberlain in the House of Commons on May 15, 1922, taken from the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">New York Times.</hi><anchor n="12" xml:id="d818-f3"
                    /></p>
                <p>Permit me to again express my view of the seriousness of the decision that you
                    are called upon to make. Our interests in the Near East are the result of
                    generations of effort and have engaged, I may say, the Christian sentiment of
                    the entire country. If our failure to take part in this inquiry and thus,
                    without any further commitment on our part, to exercise a restraining influence,
                    should lead to the virtual expulsion of our Christian missionaries and educators
                    from Asia Minor it would be most unfortunate.</p>
                <p>Permit me also to say that our refusal to take part in this inquiry will make, I
                    fear, a very disagreeable impression not only here but also with that important
                    section of opinion abroad which is most favorably disposed toward the United
                    States.</p>
                <p>I may add that the correspondence with the Department indicates a deep and
                    widespread interest in this matter.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles E. Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
